(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-pd1-exam2-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"PD1 Exam 2 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form A","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"During the health history for the anorectal exam, hematochezia refers to...","choices":["absence of stool for several days","straining during defecation","black, tarry stool","visible bloody stool"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hematochezia is the passage of visibly bloody stool, one of the key symptoms specifically asked about in the anorectal health history, along with pain on defecation and perianal lesions. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"During the male GU (genitourinary) health history, asking about painful or irregular erections with curvature specifically screens for...","choices":["epididymitis","benign prostatic hyperplasia","Peyronie's disease","testicular torsion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Painful or irregular erections with curvature is a classic history clue for Peyronie's disease, a condition involving fibrous plaque formation in the penile shaft that causes abnormal curvature during erection. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"The 'new order' of the abdominal exam sequence taught in lecture is...","choices":["Palpation, Percussion, Auscultation, Inspection","Auscultation, Inspection, Palpation, Percussion","Inspection, Palpation, Percussion, Auscultation","Inspection, Auscultation, Percussion, Palpation"],"answer":3,"exp":"The taught sequence is inspection first, then auscultation, then percussion, then palpation — auscultation is deliberately performed before percussion/palpation so that bowel sounds aren't artificially altered by prior manipulation of the abdomen. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"The angle of Louis (sternal angle) is a useful anatomic landmark because it...","choices":["marks the 2nd rib/intercostal space and corresponds to the tracheal bifurcation and upper border of the atria","has no relationship whatsoever to counting ribs or locating any intercostal spaces at all","is located at the level of the umbilicus, well below the entire thoracic cavity itself","marks the 5th rib exclusively, with no relation to any deeper internal thoracic structures"],"answer":0,"exp":"The angle of Louis is continuous with the 2nd rib, providing a starting point to count ribs/intercostal spaces to localize findings; it also marks the level where the trachea bifurcates into the main bronchi and corresponds to the upper border of the cardiac atria. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"The skin is described as the heaviest single organ, making up approximately what percentage of total body weight?","choices":["50%","90%","1%","16%"],"answer":3,"exp":"The skin is the heaviest single organ in the body, making up approximately 16% of total body weight. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Equipment needed for a comprehensive eye exam includes an eye chart, pupil gauge, pen light, and...","choices":["a stethoscope","a reflex hammer","an ophthalmoscope","a nasal speculum"],"answer":2,"exp":"The listed equipment for a comprehensive eye exam includes an eye chart, pupil gauge, pen light, and an ophthalmoscope for fundoscopic examination. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"The OPQRST mnemonic used to characterize pain history stands for onset, palliative/provocative factors, quality, radiation, severity, and...","choices":["temperature","timing","tenderness","treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"OPQRST covers Onset, Palliative/Provocative factors, Quality, Radiation, Severity, and Timing — the seven features used to systematically characterize a pain complaint. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which structure separates the right ventricle from the left ventricle?","choices":["Chordae tendineae fibers anchoring the papillary muscles","Anterior papillary muscles anchoring the valve leaflets","Interventricular septum","The interatrial septum wall separating the atria"],"answer":2,"exp":"The interventricular septum is the muscular wall separating the right and left ventricles. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"When inspecting the head, which features should be specifically noted?","choices":["Only whether or not the patient currently reports any headache symptoms","Only the patient's measured visual acuity result in each eye","Only the patient's current blood pressure and pulse rate readings","Size, shape, and contour of the skull, noting any deformity or lumps"],"answer":3,"exp":"Head inspection specifically includes assessing size, shape, and contour of the skull for deformity or lumps. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"The standard positioning for the anorectal examination has the patient...","choices":["standing upright and bending forward sharply at the waist","lying supine with both legs extended fully straight","lying prone, face down, with both arms at the sides","lying on their left side with hips and knees partially flexed"],"answer":3,"exp":"The anorectal exam is classically performed with the patient in the left lateral (side-lying) position with hips and knees partially flexed, allowing good visualization and access to the perianal area. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Lower urinary tract symptoms, one of the screened history items, would include complaints such as...","choices":["chest pain, shortness of breath, or exertional palpitations","urinary hesitancy, weak stream, frequency, or nocturia","blurred vision, visual field loss, or eye pain","joint pain, morning stiffness, and visible swelling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lower urinary tract symptoms encompass a cluster of voiding complaints including hesitancy, weak stream, urinary frequency, and nocturia, often prompting further evaluation for conditions like BPH (benign prostatic hyperplasia). — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Auscultation is performed before percussion and palpation in the abdominal exam specifically because...","choices":["auscultation is simply faster to perform and therefore saves overall clinical exam time","patients generally tolerate auscultation better when it is performed last, after palpation","percussion and palpation are technically not standard parts of the abdominal exam sequence","percussing or palpating first can alter bowel sound activity, giving a misleading auscultation result"],"answer":3,"exp":"Mechanically stimulating the bowel via percussion or palpation can transiently increase peristalsis/bowel sounds, so auscultation is performed first to get an accurate baseline assessment of bowel activity. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"To locate the angle of Louis, the examiner starts at the sternal notch and...","choices":["walks the fingers down the manubrium a few centimeters until feeling a distinct bony ridge","presses directly over the xiphoid process at the very base of the sternum","moves laterally to the midclavicular line immediately, without further palpation","counts ribs starting from the 12th rib and working upward toward the sternum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Starting at the sternal notch, the examiner walks the fingers down the manubrium a few centimeters to feel the distinct bony ridge that is the sternal angle (angle of Louis). — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"The skin's listed functions include retaining body fluids, protecting underlying tissues, modulating temperature, and...","choices":["producing insulin","synthesizing vitamin D","filtering blood","producing red blood cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Among its key functions, the skin synthesizes vitamin D (via UV-light-driven synthesis), in addition to retaining body fluids, protecting underlying tissue, and modulating temperature. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"The lateral rectus muscle, innervated by cranial nerve VI (abducens), functions to...","choices":["move the eye downward, toward the chin (depression)","move the eye outward, away from the nose (abduction)","move the eye upward, toward the brow (elevation)","move the eye inward, toward the nose (adduction)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lateral rectus, innervated by CN (cranial nerve) VI, abducts the eye (moves it outward, away from the nose) — the classic mnemonic 'LR6' reflects this pairing. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"Acute pain is typically defined as lasting less than approximately...","choices":["3 to 6 months, resolving as the injury heals","10 years","there is no time-based definition of acute pain","1 hour"],"answer":0,"exp":"Acute pain is typically defined as lasting less than about 3-6 months and resolving as the underlying injury heals, in contrast to chronic pain, which persists beyond a month past the expected resolution of an illness/injury or lasts more than 3-6 months. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the primary role of the papillary muscles and chordae tendineae together?","choices":["Preventing backflow of blood from the great vessels into the ventricles during ventricular relaxation","Initiating and conducting the electrical impulse that triggers each ventricular contraction","Anchoring the AV (atrioventricular) valve leaflets to prevent them from inverting/leaking during ventricular contraction","Forming the leaflet orifice that regulates flow from the atria into the ventricles during diastole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Papillary muscles anchor via chordae tendineae to prevent AV (atrioventricular) valve leaflets from prolapsing into the atria during systole. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"During face inspection, which findings should the examiner assess?","choices":["Only the size of the patient's pupils, assessed bilaterally","Asymmetry, expression, edema, scars, and involuntary movements","Only the turgor of the patient's overlying facial skin tissue","Only the strength of the patient's jaw against resistance"],"answer":1,"exp":"These are the specific face inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"The physical exam of the anus and rectum proceeds through which sequence of components?","choices":["Only the DRE (digital rectal examination) is performed; visual inspection is not part of this exam","Perform the DRE (digital rectal examination) first, then inspect the anus and perianal areas afterward","Inspect the sacrococcygeal/perianal areas, inspect the anus, then perform a DRE (digital rectal examination)","Palpate the rectal wall first via DRE (digital rectal examination), then inspect the perianal areas and anus afterward"],"answer":2,"exp":"The exam proceeds from external inspection (sacrococcygeal and perianal areas, then the anus itself) before moving to the internal DRE (digital rectal examination). — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"If a patient develops an erection during the genital exam, the recommended examiner response is to...","choices":["calmly explain that it is a normal response, complete the exam, and maintain a composed demeanor","scold the patient sharply and visibly for having this entirely involuntary physiologic reaction","immediately stop the entire exam and leave the room abruptly without offering any explanation","document it as a definite abnormal finding that requires urgent specialist referral"],"answer":0,"exp":"An erection during the exam is a normal physiologic response; the examiner should calmly reassure the patient, complete the necessary examination, and maintain a professional, composed demeanor throughout. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Purple striae noted on abdominal inspection can be associated with...","choices":["a completely normal finding in all patients","only recent weight loss with no other significance","normal aging with no clinical correlation","liver disease and dilated veins (caput medusae)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Purple striae are associated with conditions like liver disease and can accompany the dilated periumbilical veins known as caput medusae, both of which may be visible on abdominal inspection. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"When taking a review of systems related to a respiratory complaint, key elements to characterize a cough include its onset, nature, pattern, severity, and...","choices":["only their family's insurance status","associated symptoms","only the patient's occupation","only their preferred pharmacy"],"answer":1,"exp":"A thorough cough history characterizes onset, nature, pattern, severity, and any associated symptoms (for example, fever, weight loss, hemoptysis), which together help build the differential. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"The three layers of the skin, from most superficial to deepest, are the epidermis, dermis, and...","choices":["muscularis mucosae layer","the periosteum layer","subcutaneous tissue","the visceral pleura"],"answer":2,"exp":"The three skin layers, from superficial to deep, are the epidermis (containing the stratum corneum, stratum basale, and stratum spinosum), the dermis (containing glands, hair follicles, and nerve terminals), and the subcutaneous tissue. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"The superior oblique muscle, innervated by cranial nerve IV (trochlear), functions to...","choices":["have no meaningful role whatsoever in normal extraocular eye movement","rotate the top of the eye toward the nose (intorsion) and also move the eye downward (depression)","move the eye inward, toward the nose (adduction), with no torsional component","move the eye outward, away from the nose (abduction), and rotate it upward"],"answer":1,"exp":"The superior oblique, innervated by CN (cranial nerve) IV, produces intorsion (rotating the top of the eye toward the nose around the long axis) and also contributes to depression of the eye — the classic 'SO4' pairing. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"Nociceptive pain, one of the two general pain categories discussed, results from...","choices":["tissue damage, such as to skin, musculoskeletal structures, or viscera","psychological factors exclusively, with no underlying physical basis","normal, non-painful sensory processing of ordinary everyday stimuli","damage or disease affecting the nervous system itself, as in neuropathic pain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Nociceptive pain arises from actual or potential tissue damage (skin, musculoskeletal, or visceral), detected by nociceptors, in contrast to neuropathic pain, which results from damage or disease affecting the nervous system itself. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which valve regulates blood flow from the left atrium into the left ventricle?","choices":["Pulmonic valve","Aortic valve","Mitral valve","Tricuspid valve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The mitral valve sits between the left atrium and left ventricle and regulates this flow. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"During palpation of the head, what should the examiner assess?","choices":["Only the range of motion of the patient's cervical neck","Only auscultation for bruits overlying the scalp and skull","Only the patient's peripheral nail-bed capillary refill time","Areas of tenderness, deformity, masses, and hair texture"],"answer":3,"exp":"These are the specific findings assessed on head palpation per the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"During inspection of the anus and perianal region, the examiner should specifically look for...","choices":["bowel sound frequency and pitch, assessed during abdominal auscultation","lesions, ulcers, inflammation, rashes, excoriations, and external skin breakdown","liver span measured by careful abdominal percussion technique alone","blood pressure and pulse, measured routinely as part of the vital signs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Inspection of the anus and rectum region specifically screens for lesions, ulcers, inflammation, rashes, excoriations, and other external skin breakdown, as well as masses or tenderness, before any palpation. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"To help address patient apprehension before beginning the genital exam, the examiner should...","choices":["ask the patient to reschedule the entire visit if they appear nervous or hesitant","skip any explanation at all in order to save time during a busy clinic day","proceed immediately with the exam without ever discussing the process beforehand","thoroughly explain the examination process and, if needed, have an assistant present"],"answer":3,"exp":"Thoroughly explaining what the exam will involve helps reassure and inform the patient, and having an assistant (chaperone) present when needed provides additional comfort and appropriate practice safeguards. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"On abdominal inspection, a pulsation visible in the epigastrium is most likely to represent...","choices":["normal aortic pulsation, which can be visible in thin patients","a definite sign of active bowel obstruction requiring urgent workup","always an abnormal finding requiring immediate surgical intervention","never a normal finding, regardless of the patient's body habitus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Aortic pulsation may be normally visible in the epigastrium, particularly in thin patients, and is a recognized normal inspection finding distinct from abnormal peristaltic waves seen in obstruction. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Among the listed risk factors for pulmonary disease, which grouping is accurate?","choices":["None of the listed factors have any meaningful effect whatsoever on pulmonary disease risk","Only consistently high levels of regular physical activity, with no other contributing factors","Only a young age and female gender, with no other listed contributing risk factors","Male gender, older age, smoking, sedentary lifestyle, occupational exposure, obesity, and recurrent chest infections"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture lists these specific risk factors for pulmonary disease: male gender (more than female), older age, smoking, sedentary lifestyle, occupational exposure, obesity, and a history of recurrent chest infections. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which structures are located within the dermis layer of the skin?","choices":["Only red and yellow bone marrow tissue, with nothing else present","Only the stratum corneum, which is actually part of the epidermis instead","Sebaceous glands, sweat glands, hair follicles, and cutaneous nerve terminals","Nothing at all; the dermis is a completely inert, structureless layer"],"answer":2,"exp":"The dermis (the middle layer, deep to the epidermis) contains sebaceous glands, sweat glands, hair follicles, and cutaneous nerve terminals. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve innervates all extraocular muscles except the lateral rectus (CN (cranial nerve) VI) and superior oblique (CN (cranial nerve) IV)?","choices":["Cranial nerve III (oculomotor)","Cranial nerve II (optic)","Cranial nerve V (trigeminal)","Cranial nerve VII (facial)"],"answer":0,"exp":"CN (cranial nerve) III (oculomotor) innervates the superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, and inferior oblique — all the extraocular muscles except the lateral rectus (CN (cranial nerve) VI, abducens) and superior oblique (CN (cranial nerve) IV, trochlear), following the classic 'SO4 LR6, all the rest CN3' mnemonic. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"When using a 1-10 conceptual pain scale, an important technique tip is to...","choices":["ask the patient what their personal '10' reference point is (for example, childbirth, a fracture)","assume that every patient's personal '10' means the exact same underlying intensity of pain","use only a compressed 1-to-3 scale for every patient, never the full 1-to-10 range","never ask the patient about their personal reference point for comparison"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because pain is subjective, asking the patient what experience represents their personal '10' (for example, childbirth, a fracture, a paper cut) helps the examiner better interpret and contextualize the numeric rating they give for their current pain. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"SA (sinoatrial) node normally generates impulses at what rate?","choices":["40–60 times per minute","100–160 times per minute","20–40 times per minute","60–100 times per minute"],"answer":3,"exp":"The SA (sinoatrial) node is the heart's natural pacemaker, generating impulses at a rate of 60–100 beats per minute. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which structure of the tympanic membrane is the small, loose portion located above the malleus?","choices":["Pars tensa","Pars flaccida","Umbo","Cone of light"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pars flaccida is the small, loose superior portion of the tympanic membrane. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"DRE (digital rectal examination), after lubricating the gloved index finger and inserting the fingertip into the anal canal, the examiner should next ask the patient to...","choices":["hold their breath for 30 seconds","cough forcefully","stand up immediately","squeeze the anal sphincter"],"answer":3,"exp":"After the fingertip is inserted, the examiner asks the patient to squeeze the anal sphincter around the finger, which allows assessment of sphincter tone, before proceeding to palpate the rectal surface more broadly. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"When the patient is supine for the genital exam, proper draping technique involves...","choices":["keeping the entire body fully covered at all times, including whichever specific area is being actively examined","using a gown to cover the chest/abdomen and placing a drape at the midthigh, exposing only the genitalia/inguinal area as needed","completely undressing the patient entirely, with no gown or drape used anywhere throughout the exam","draping and gowning are considered entirely unnecessary for this particular type of intimate genital exam"],"answer":1,"exp":"Proper technique covers the chest and abdomen with a gown and places a drape at the midthigh, exposing only the genitalia and inguinal areas actually being examined at any given moment, preserving patient modesty. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Normal bowel sounds are described as clicks and gurgles occurring at a rate of approximately...","choices":["1 to 2 per hour","5 to 34 per minute","exactly 10 per minute, with no variation","100 to 200 per minute"],"answer":1,"exp":"Normal bowel sounds (clicks and gurgles) occur at a rate of roughly 5 to 34 per minute, a fairly wide normal range. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"On inspection of the thorax, the examiner should assess shape and symmetry, A/P diameter, and screen for...","choices":["scoliosis, kyphosis, tracheal displacement, and pectus deformities","only heart murmurs detected on cardiac auscultation exam","only the patient's measured resting blood pressure value","only bowel sounds heard during abdominal auscultation exam"],"answer":0,"exp":"Thoracic inspection includes screening for spinal deformities (scoliosis, kyphosis), tracheal displacement, and chest wall deformities such as pectus excavatum or pectus carinatum. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"Skin color variation depends on several pigments, including melanin (a brownish pigment) and hemoglobin. Oxyhemoglobin, found in arteries/capillaries, produces a...","choices":["darker bluish color","yellow color","bright red color","no color change at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Oxyhemoglobin, the oxygenated form of hemoglobin found in arteries and capillaries, produces a bright red pigment, in contrast to deoxyhemoglobin (found in veins), which has a darker, bluish color. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"Before testing visual acuity with a Snellen wall chart, the patient should be positioned at a distance of...","choices":["50 feet","100 feet","6 inches","20 feet"],"answer":3,"exp":"The standard Snellen wall chart is used at a distance of 20 feet, while the smaller Snellen card is used at 6 feet and a handheld card at 14 inches — always checking the specific chart's printed instructions for its intended distance. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"The general survey begins with the examiner's initial impression of the patient upon entering the room, paying particular attention to appearance, apparent state of health, discomfort/distress, skin color, dress, grooming/hygiene, facial expression/affect, posture, gait, and...","choices":["visual acuity number","mobility/motor activity","nothing beyond appearance","blood type"],"answer":1,"exp":"The general survey checklist includes mobility and motor activity, alongside appearance, apparent state of health, distress, skin color, dress, grooming, affect, posture, and gait — forming a broad initial impression before the focused exam begins. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which node delays the electrical impulse low in the atrial septum before it continues down the bundle of His?","choices":["Bundle of His pathway","SA (sinoatrial) node","AV (atrioventricular) node","Purkinje fiber network"],"answer":2,"exp":"The AV (atrioventricular) node delays the impulse, allowing the ventricles time to fill, before conduction continues down the bundle of His. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"When inspecting the tympanic membrane with the otoscope, which features should be assessed?","choices":["Only the presence of visible cerumen within the ear canal","Only tenderness elicited over the mastoid process itself","Color, contour, injection, perforation, opacities, and distortions","Only the symmetry of the two external auricles bilaterally"],"answer":2,"exp":"These are the specific TM (tympanic membrane) inspection findings listed, along with landmarks such as the malleus, umbo, pars tensa/flaccida, and cone of light. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Among the examples of anorectal abnormalities the exam may detect, which grouping is correct?","choices":["Cardiac murmurs, diastolic gallops, and pericardial friction rubs heard on auscultation","Abdominal borborygmi and vascular bruits heard on routine abdominal auscultation exams","Hemorrhoids, anal fissures or warts, sphincter tightness or laxity, perianal abscesses, and malignancies","Pulmonary wheezes, rhonchi, and inspiratory crackles heard on routine lung auscultation"],"answer":2,"exp":"These are the specific examples of anorectal abnormalities listed: hemorrhoids, anal fissures/warts, abnormal sphincter tone (tightness or laxity), perianal abscesses, and malignancies. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"The standard sequence of the male genital physical exam includes inspecting the penis, urethral meatus, and scrotum, then...","choices":["auscultating the scrotum and inguinal region with a stethoscope before any palpation begins","palpating the shaft of the penis, assessing for groin hernias, and palpating the epididymis and each testis","only continuing visual inspection further, with no palpation ever performed at any point","immediately performing a DRE (digital rectal examination), skipping every other exam step entirely"],"answer":1,"exp":"After inspection, the exam proceeds to palpation: the penile shaft, assessment for groin hernias (inspection and palpation without and with Valsalva), and palpation of the epididymis and each testis, followed by examination of the spermatic cord. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Borborygmi refers to...","choices":["a coarse grating friction rub heard with respiration over the liver surface","a whooshing vascular bruit heard with the diaphragm over the aorta","prolonged gurgles of hyperperistalsis, informally known as 'stomach growling'","the complete and prolonged absence of any audible bowel sounds at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Borborygmi are prolonged, loud gurgling sounds reflecting hyperperistalsis — what's commonly called 'stomach growling.' — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"Pectus excavatum, one of the chest wall deformities screened for on inspection, is best described as...","choices":["a normal variant present in nearly every patient","a protrusion or outward bulging of the sternum","a finding detected only through chest auscultation","a depression/sunken appearance of the sternum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pectus excavatum is a chest wall deformity in which the sternum appears sunken or depressed inward, in contrast to pectus carinatum, where the sternum protrudes outward. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"Deoxyhemoglobin, found in veins, contributes a darker pigment with what color quality compared with oxyhemoglobin?","choices":["a bright red color","an identical color to oxyhemoglobin","a bluish color","a bright yellow color"],"answer":2,"exp":"Deoxyhemoglobin (the deoxygenated form found in veins) has a darker pigment with a bluish quality, in contrast to the bright red color of oxyhemoglobin in arteries/capillaries. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"When testing visual acuity, a handheld eye chart is typically used at a distance of...","choices":["six feet","20 feet","one foot","14 inches"],"answer":3,"exp":"A handheld visual acuity card is typically used at 14 inches, compared with 20 feet for the wall-mounted Snellen chart and 6 feet for the Snellen card, though the printed instructions on any specific chart should always be checked. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"The traditional vital signs include blood pressure, heart rate, respiratory rate, and temperature; which additional measurement is increasingly included when available, though not traditionally classified as a vital sign?","choices":["pulse oximetry","hearing threshold","grip strength","visual acuity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pulse oximetry (measuring blood oxygen saturation) is increasingly included alongside the four traditional vital signs (BP (blood pressure), heart rate, respiratory rate, temperature) when the equipment is available, even though it isn't traditionally classified as one of the core vital signs. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"Cardiac output is best defined as:","choices":["The percentage of end-diastolic ventricular volume ejected with each single heartbeat, known as ejection fraction","The volume of blood ejected from each ventricle in one minute, equal to heart rate times stroke volume","The volume of blood ejected from the ventricle with a single heartbeat, known as the stroke volume","The pressure the ventricle must generate to overcome resistance and eject blood, known as afterload"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume, representing the volume ejected per minute. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"When using the otoscope to examine the ear, how should the auricle be manipulated to straighten the canal?","choices":["Push the auricle flat and firmly against the side of the scalp","Pull the auricle up, back, and slightly away from the head","Pull the auricle downward and forward, toward the patient's face","No manipulation of the auricle is needed at any point"],"answer":1,"exp":"This maneuver straightens the ear canal, which is naturally curved, for better otoscopic visualization. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"Special techniques and maneuvers associated with the anorectal exam include performing a prostatic examination and a rectovaginal examination, the latter of which is performed in which position?","choices":["Prone position","Lithotomy position","Trendelenburg position","Left lateral (side-lying) position"],"answer":1,"exp":"The rectovaginal examination, one of the special maneuvers associated with the anorectal exam, is performed with the patient in the lithotomy position. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Assessing for a groin hernia involves inspection and palpation performed...","choices":["only in the standing position, never supine","only with the patient lying flat and relaxed, never straining","only by auscultation","both without and with the Valsalva maneuver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hernia assessment specifically involves examining the groin both at rest and with the patient performing a Valsalva maneuver (bearing down), since increased intra-abdominal pressure can reveal a hernia bulge not otherwise apparent. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"If bowel sounds appear absent on initial listening, the examiner should...","choices":["immediately document 'absent' after only a few brief seconds of silence","listen for one full minute in each quadrant before documenting them as truly absent","switch immediately to percussion instead, abandoning further auscultation","skip auscultation of the remaining three quadrants entirely and move on"],"answer":1,"exp":"Bowel sounds can be intermittent, so the examiner must listen for a full minute in each of the four quadrants before concluding and documenting that bowel sounds are truly absent. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Assessing chest expansion (lung/respiratory excursion) is performed at approximately the level of the...","choices":["the 2nd rib exclusively, with the examiner's fingers pressed flat against the sternum","10th rib, with the examiner's fingers loosely grasping the rib cage while the patient inhales deeply","the clavicle exclusively, with the examiner's fingers hooked directly over the bone","the xiphoid process only, with no specific rib-level reference point being used"],"answer":1,"exp":"Chest expansion is assessed at about the level of the 10th rib, with the examiner's thumbs meeting near the spine and fingers loosely grasping the lateral rib cage; the patient inhales deeply while the examiner watches the distance the thumbs move apart. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"Carotene, one of the pigments affecting skin color, is a yellow pigment found in...","choices":["only within circulating red blood cells","found only within the hair follicle shaft","only within the epidermis' deepest stratum basale layer","subcutaneous fat and heavily keratinized areas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Carotene is a yellow pigment found in subcutaneous fat and heavily keratinized areas of skin (such as the palms and soles). — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"When performing visual acuity testing, the correct technique is to...","choices":["test both eyes together only the entire time, without ever occluding either eye individually","determine and test only the patient's dominant eye, skipping the non-dominant eye entirely","cover one eye and read the smallest line possible, then repeat with the other eye, then repeat with both eyes open","always test acuity without any regard whatsoever to the patient's corrective lenses or contacts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Visual acuity testing covers one eye at a time (reading the smallest legible line), then repeats with the other eye, and finally repeats with both eyes open, checking with and without corrective lenses if applicable. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 17"}]};
